const STUDY_GUIDES = {"lesson-1":{"title":"Nutrition Basics","subtitle":"Learn the concepts, master the calculations, and prepare for all 25 supplied test questions.","source":"Comprehensive test-aligned teaching guide based on your supplied NC7 Lesson Test 1 and linked primary sources. The full assigned textbook chapter and official answer key were not supplied. Historical label conventions and ambiguous test wording are explicitly flagged. Original practice is separate from the source test.","comprehensive":true,"sections":[{"id":"l1-1","title":"How to use this guide","html":"<p>This guide teaches the foundations behind all 25 questions in your uploaded <strong>NC7 Lesson Test 1</strong>. It is deliberately more than a short summary: the goal is to explain the ideas well enough that you can solve a differently worded question, not just recognize a letter.</p>\n<p><strong>Scope matters:</strong> the uploaded test concerns nutrition basics. This is a test-aligned teaching guide, not a verified, exhaustive summary of the assigned textbook chapter; the full chapter and an official answer key were not supplied. Some questions use older American labelling conventions or imprecise wording. Those places are flagged rather than disguised as certain answers. The textbook screenshot about counselling skills is not a substitute for this nutrition content.</p>\n<p>Study in three passes. First, read for understanding and explain each major idea aloud. Second, redo the calculations without looking. Third, complete the original practice questions near the end, check the explanations, and return only to weak areas. Keep two columns in your notes: <strong>what the science means</strong> and <strong>what this particular course question appears to be testing</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"l1-2","title":"1 The nutrient framework","html":"<p>A nutrient is a substance the body uses for functions such as providing energy, building or repairing structures, or regulating processes. An <strong>essential nutrient</strong> must be obtained through the diet because the body cannot make it, or cannot make enough to meet its needs. Essential does not mean that larger amounts are always better. It also does not mean every component of food is essential.</p>\n<p>The six familiar nutrient classes are carbohydrates, lipids or fats, proteins, vitamins, minerals, and water. Carbohydrate, protein, and fat are the three <strong>energy yielding macronutrients</strong>. Vitamins and minerals are <strong>micronutrients</strong>: needed in smaller quantities, not of smaller importance. Water is needed in large amounts but supplies no Calories. Some classifications include water among macronutrients; the test&#x27;s energy calculations concern carbohydrate, protein, and fat only.</p>\n<p>Think of the body as needing <strong>fuel, building materials, and operating conditions</strong>. Carbohydrate supplies usable fuel; protein supplies amino acids for tissues, enzymes, and many other molecules; fats supply concentrated energy and components of membranes. Vitamins and minerals assist many processes, while water provides a suitable environment and transport system. These functions overlap. Protein can supply energy, but that does not make energy its only purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>No Calories does not mean no importance.</strong> A vitamin can help an enzyme release energy from food without providing that energy itself. Likewise, water can participate in metabolism without being an energy source. This distinction prevents several common test errors.</p>\n<p>Cholesterol illustrates another distinction: a substance can be useful in the body without being an essential dietary nutrient. The body synthesizes cholesterol. A food&#x27;s containing it does not make it the best answer to a question asking which essential nutrient that food adds.</p>"},{"id":"l1-3","title":"2 Reference values and what they actually mean","html":"<p>Dietary Reference Intakes, or <strong>DRIs</strong>, are a family of reference values, not one universal recommended number. The crucial question is always: <strong>What job is this particular reference value doing?</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning to learn</th><th scope=\"col\">Main distinction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EAR</td><td>Intake estimated to meet the requirement of half a specified healthy group</td><td>A midpoint of requirements, not a target sufficient for everyone</td></tr><tr><td>RDA</td><td>Intake intended to meet nearly all healthy individuals&#x27; requirements in a specified group</td><td>About 97–98 percent, not the same as EAR</td></tr><tr><td>AI</td><td>Intake estimate used when evidence cannot establish an EAR and RDA</td><td>An alternative reference, not a provisional low RDA</td></tr><tr><td>UL</td><td>Highest usual intake unlikely to cause adverse effects in almost everyone in the specified group</td><td>A safety boundary, not a goal</td></tr><tr><td>EER</td><td>Estimated energy intake needed for energy balance, considering relevant personal characteristics</td><td>About energy needs, not a vitamin recommendation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These definitions concern appropriate life-stage and sex groups and generally healthy populations. <strong>Memory aid: EAR = half; RDA = almost all; AI = alternative; UL = upper safety limit.</strong> <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada DRI definitions</a></p>\n<p>An EAR does not mean a person should deliberately consume only half their nutrient requirement. It means that people&#x27;s requirements differ: at the EAR, the amount would cover approximately half of the relevant group. That is why test question 1 contrasts “half” with “98 percent.”</p>\n<p>An RDA is not a diagnostic threshold. Eating less than it on one day does not prove deficiency. Nor does exceeding it automatically mean toxicity. Nutritional status depends on usual intake, absorption, stores, and other circumstances. A UL is not an instruction to take a supplement up to that number. Some ULs apply to particular sources rather than every form of a nutrient; check the actual reference before using a number clinically. <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222891/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">National Academies guidance on individual assessment</a></p>\n<p><strong>Daily Value, or DV, is different.</strong> It is a standardized reference used on food labels. A label is designed for comparison across products, not to calculate your personal requirement precisely. If a serving supplies 20 percent DV for calcium, it contributes one fifth of the label reference. It does not prove that it supplies exactly one fifth of your own requirement. <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-facts-label/how-understand-and-use-nutrition-facts-label\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA guide to interpreting labels</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-4","title":"3 Carbohydrates and the sugar building blocks","html":"<p>A <strong>monosaccharide</strong> is one sugar unit. Learn glucose, fructose, and galactose. A <strong>disaccharide</strong> contains two monosaccharide units joined together. The word parts help: mono means one, di means two.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Disaccharide</th><th scope=\"col\">Building blocks</th><th scope=\"col\">Familiar association</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sucrose</td><td>Glucose and fructose</td><td>Table sugar</td></tr><tr><td>Lactose</td><td>Glucose and galactose</td><td>Milk sugar</td></tr><tr><td>Maltose</td><td>Glucose and glucose</td><td>Starch breakdown</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For question 2, recognize the structure, not just the word sugar. Lactose and maltose are themselves disaccharides, so an option pairing glucose with either of them cannot correctly name sucrose&#x27;s two monosaccharides. <strong>Memory aid: sucrose pairs glucose with fructose; milk adds galactose; maltose repeats glucose.</strong></p>\n<p>Starch is made of many glucose units. Dietary fibre includes carbohydrate components that resist digestion by human digestive enzymes; some are fermented by intestinal microbes. For the basic arithmetic in this test, use the supplied total carbohydrate grams and the conventional 4 kcal per gram factor. Real food labelling may handle particular fibres and other components differently, so simple textbook calculations can differ slightly from the label&#x27;s Calories.</p>\n<p>Sugar being a carbohydrate does not mean every carbohydrate is sugar, and sugar being present does not describe the entire nutritional value of a food. Milk, fruit, and a sweetened beverage can all contain sugars while differing greatly in protein, fibre, vitamins, and minerals. Read the whole food context.</p>"},{"id":"l1-5","title":"4 Energy calculations you should be able to do independently","html":"<p>In food discussions, a capital C <strong>Calorie</strong> means a kilocalorie, or kcal. The test uses these interchangeably. Memorize <strong>4–4–9</strong>: carbohydrate supplies approximately 4 kcal per gram, protein 4, and fat 9.</p>\n<p>Use this sequence every time:</p>\n<ol><li>Identify what amount the nutrient grams describe: one serving, the whole recipe, or a whole day.</li><li>Multiply each macronutrient&#x27;s grams by its own energy factor.</li><li>Add the three energy amounts to obtain total energy.</li><li>Adjust for the fraction actually eaten, if needed.</li><li>For a percentage, divide one nutrient&#x27;s energy by total energy, then multiply by 100.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong> total kcal = carbohydrate grams × 4 + protein grams × 4 + fat grams × 9.</p>\n<p>Do not divide grams by Calories. The numerator and denominator must be in the same energy unit before the ratio becomes an energy percentage. Do not assume equal gram weights contribute equal energy: 10 g fat supplies 90 kcal, while 10 g carbohydrate supplies 40 kcal.</p>\n<h4>Worked example 1 The smoothie in question 7</h4>\n<p>Protein: 9 g × 4 = 36 kcal. Carbohydrate: 102 g × 4 = 408 kcal. Fat: 1 g × 9 = 9 kcal. Total: 36 + 408 + 9 = <strong>453 kcal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Why this works: each nutrient contributes its own portion to the total. Adding 9 + 102 + 1 gives grams of macronutrients, not Calories. Multiplying all 112 g by 4 would incorrectly treat fat as carbohydrate or protein.</p>\n<h4>Worked example 2 The half sandwich in question 8</h4>\n<p>The entire sandwich contains 20 g carbohydrate, 8 g protein, and 5 g fat. Its energy is 80 + 32 + 45 = <strong>157 kcal</strong>. The person eats half, so 157 ÷ 2 = <strong>78.5 kcal</strong>, approximately 80.</p>\n<p>You could instead halve every nutrient first: 10 g carbohydrate, 4 g protein, and 2.5 g fat yield 40 + 16 + 22.5 = 78.5. Both methods agree. A result near 160 describes the whole sandwich, not the portion eaten.</p>\n<h4>Worked example 3 The label in question 6</h4>\n<p>The supplied label lists 170 kcal and 5 g fat. Fat energy is 5 × 9 = 45 kcal, also printed on this older label. Percentage of energy from fat = 45 ÷ 170 × 100 = <strong>26.5 percent</strong>, approximately.</p>\n<p>The printed fat percent DV is not the number to use. Percent DV and percent energy have different denominators. Also, the label&#x27;s displayed grams yield 173 kcal using simple 4–4–9 arithmetic. Small discrepancies can arise from rounding and energy calculation conventions. For this question, use its stated 170 kcal total rather than replacing it with a newly calculated total.</p>\n<h4>Worked example 4 Jill in questions 16 to 18</h4>\n<p>Jill consumes 250 g carbohydrate, 100 g total fat, and 50 g protein. Her 30 g saturated fat is <strong>already part of the 100 g total fat</strong>. Never count it a second time.</p>\n<p>Carbohydrate: 250 × 4 = <strong>1,000 kcal</strong>. Fat: 100 × 9 = <strong>900 kcal</strong>. Protein: 50 × 4 = <strong>200 kcal</strong>. Total: <strong>2,100 kcal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carbohydrate percentage: 1,000 ÷ 2,100 × 100 = <strong>47.6 percent</strong>, rounding to 48. Fat percentage: 900 ÷ 2,100 × 100 = <strong>42.9 percent</strong>, rounding to 43. Protein percentage: 200 ÷ 2,100 × 100 = about 9.5. The unrounded percentages add to 100.</p>\n<p>Her weight and activity provide context, but they are not needed for these arithmetic questions. Maintaining weight does not prove that every nutrient intake is adequate or that the macronutrient balance is appropriate. Energy balance and nutrient adequacy are related but different questions.</p>\n<h4>Transfer example Working backwards</h4>\n<p>A hypothetical 2,000 kcal diet contains 30 percent of its energy from fat. Fat energy is 2,000 × 0.30 = 600 kcal. Fat grams are 600 ÷ 9 = about <strong>66.7 g</strong>. Multiply by the percentage to find energy; divide by the energy factor to return to grams. This is not an individualized diet prescription.</p>"},{"id":"l1-6","title":"5 Macronutrient ranges and age","html":"<p>The <strong>Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range</strong>, or AMDR, describes the share of total dietary energy supplied by a macronutrient. It is not a percentage of food weight and not a label DV.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Age group</th><th scope=\"col\">Carbohydrate</th><th scope=\"col\">Protein</th><th scope=\"col\">Fat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adults 19 and older</td><td>45–65 percent</td><td>10–35 percent</td><td>20–35 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Children and teenagers 4–18</td><td>45–65 percent</td><td>10–30 percent</td><td>25–35 percent</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These age-specific ranges are published in <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-macronutrients.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada&#x27;s macronutrient reference table</a>.</p>\n<p>The label&#x27;s 26.5 percent energy from fat lies within both ranges shown. However, AMDRs concern the <strong>overall diet</strong>, not a requirement that each individual food match them. Oil is mostly fat; fruit is largely carbohydrate. A balanced pattern can include both.</p>\n<p>Assuming Jill is under 19, use the 4–18 row when interpreting her intake; a 19-year-old uses the adult row. Her carbohydrate percentage is within either range, while her fat percentage is above both. Her protein percentage is slightly below either range. Do not infer a clinical deficiency from this information alone, and do not impose an adult weight-loss interpretation on a growing teenager.</p>"},{"id":"l1-7","title":"6 Reading labels without mixing up different numbers","html":"<p>Start with serving size and servings consumed. If a label describes 40 g and you eat 80 g, double the stated nutrient amounts and Calories. A serving size is the basis of the label calculation, not automatically a recommendation for how much you personally should eat. <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-facts-label/serving-size-nutrition-facts-label\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA serving size guidance</a></p>\n<p>Next, distinguish <strong>amount</strong>, <strong>percent DV</strong>, and <strong>percent energy</strong>. A hypothetical food could contain 8 g fat, contribute a particular percentage of the daily fat reference, and derive 40 percent of its own Calories from fat. These are three answers to three different questions.</p>\n<p>Question 3 is imprecise because it does not clearly separate a whole food label, its ingredient or allergen information, and the Nutrition Facts panel. Its wording about “the amount recommended for ingestion each day” could imply an individualized recommendation, which labels do not provide. Yet daily reference information does appear on labels. Therefore, do not treat that wording as an unambiguous scientific fact or an official keyed answer.</p>\n<p><strong>The supplied label is an older US-style example.</strong> Its vitamin A listing and “Calories from Fat” line help identify that context. Question 5 appears to target vitamin C under that older format. Current US labels require vitamin D and potassium alongside calcium and iron; vitamins A and C are generally optional, and the Calories from Fat line was removed. <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-food-labeling-and-critical-foods/changes-nutrition-facts-label\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA label changes</a></p>\n<p>For the test, recognize the historical convention. For actual shopping, use current rules in the country where the product is sold. Do not assume the mock label is a flawless regulatory model: its printed 5 g fat and 5 percent DV should not be used to infer a valid current daily fat reference.</p>"},{"id":"l1-8","title":"7 Enrichment fortification and refined grains","html":"<p><strong>Enrichment</strong> commonly refers to adding selected nutrients back after processing losses. <strong>Fortification</strong> is the broader practice of adding nutrients, including nutrients not originally present in substantial amounts. Usage overlaps in policy and everyday language, so these are learning distinctions rather than mutually exclusive chemical processes.</p>\n<p>Refining a grain removes some portions of the grain and can reduce fibre and micronutrients. Adding selected vitamins and iron does not recreate every component removed. Consequently, enriched white flour and whole-grain flour are not nutritionally identical.</p>\n<p>For question 4, learn the enrichment group: <strong>thiamin, riboflavin, niacin, and iron</strong>, with folic acid also important in modern standards. The options list the first four. A useful recall pattern is <strong>three B vitamins plus iron</strong>, then remember folic acid belongs in the broader modern picture.</p>\n<p>Do not memorize the question&#x27;s “MUST” as a universal rule for every refined-grain product everywhere. Canadian rules require enrichment of white flour and foods containing it, while product categories and jurisdictions matter. US standards specify the composition of products represented as enriched. <a href=\"https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/grain-and-bakery-products/unenriched-flour\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Canadian flour requirements</a> and <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/media/94563/download\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA fortification guidance</a></p>\n<p>Milk illustrates why a food can supply several important nutrients. Calcium is the strongest match among question 9&#x27;s options. Milk also supplies protein and other nutrients, and fortification may add vitamins. “Fortified” does not mean that every valuable nutrient in milk was added during manufacturing.</p>"},{"id":"l1-9","title":"8 Vitamins storage functions and deficiency","html":"<p>Vitamins are organic compounds required in small amounts for normal functioning. They do not supply Calories. A useful first classification is their solubility.</p>\n<p><strong>Fat soluble: A, D, E, K.</strong> Absorption is associated with fat digestion, and body storage can be substantial. Their functions differ: vitamin A is associated with vision and tissue maintenance; D with calcium regulation and bone health; E with antioxidant protection; K with blood clotting proteins, among other roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Water soluble: B vitamins and vitamin C.</strong> B vitamins participate in many metabolic processes; vitamin C has roles including collagen formation and antioxidant activity. Saying “B vitamins help energy metabolism” is not the same as saying a B-vitamin pill contains energy.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory aid: ADEK travels with fat; B and C go with water.</strong> This directly supports question 21. For question 22, understand the general storage principle: limited stores of many water-soluble vitamins can make inadequate intake show up sooner than inadequate intake of some stored fat-soluble vitamins. The statement that fat-soluble deficiency necessarily appears faster reverses that introductory generalization.</p>\n<p>But avoid turning that into an absolute rule. Vitamin B12 is water soluble and can be stored in sufficient amounts for deficiency symptoms to take years to develop. Timing also depends on initial stores, intake, absorption, losses, and the specific nutrient. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB12-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH vitamin B12 fact sheet</a></p>\n<p>Water soluble does not mean harmless at any dose. Excess supplemental vitamin B6 can damage nerves. Similarly, storage of some fat-soluble vitamins helps explain why excessive supplementation can pose risks, not why ordinary food containing those vitamins should be feared. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB6-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH vitamin B6 fact sheet</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-10","title":"9 Minerals food sources and absorption","html":"<p>Minerals are inorganic elements with structural and regulatory roles. Calcium supports bone structure and functions such as muscle contraction; iron is required in oxygen-carrying proteins; sodium helps fluid balance and electrical signalling. “Trace mineral” means a smaller required quantity, not an unimportant function.</p>\n<p><strong>Calcium:</strong> milk and other dairy foods are familiar sources; appropriately fortified alternatives and some other foods can also contribute. A food containing calcium is not automatically identical to another calcium source. The amount, serving size, and absorption matter. Oxalate in spinach binds calcium and reduces its absorption, so total calcium content alone does not tell the whole story. <a href=\"https://www.medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002412.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MedlinePlus calcium overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Iron:</strong> meat provides heme iron as well as nonheme iron; beans and fortified cereals provide nonheme iron. Heme iron is generally more readily absorbed, while nonheme absorption is more responsive to the meal. Vitamin C can improve nonheme iron absorption. Thus beans plus a vitamin C-containing vegetable illustrate how foods can complement each other. In question 25, the half-cup peaches serving is the comparatively weak iron source among the listed choices; this does not make peaches an unhealthy food. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH iron fact sheet</a></p>\n<p><strong>Sodium:</strong> processed meats and many packaged soups can contribute substantial sodium. That is the relevant connection in question 10, rather than assuming sugar or caffeine explains every blood-pressure concern. Reducing sodium within a suitable eating pattern can help lower blood pressure. Individual clinical needs require individualized advice. <a href=\"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/dash/health-benefits\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NHLBI DASH evidence</a></p>\n<p>Question 23 compares mineral absorption with vitamin absorption as if each group had a single typical percentage. That is too broad for a dependable universal ranking. <strong>Bioavailability</strong> concerns how much of an ingested nutrient becomes available for use, and varies with chemical form, food matrix, other meal components, nutrient status, and health. Learn those factors rather than inventing a percentage for “all minerals” or “all vitamins.” Ask which textbook generalization the course intends if forced to mark this statement true or false.</p>"},{"id":"l1-11","title":"10 Water and why the question needs care","html":"<p>Water is essential although it provides no energy. It serves as the medium for many chemical reactions, supports transport of substances, contributes to waste elimination, and helps temperature regulation. Sweating can cool the body through evaporation; urine carries dissolved wastes. Water also participates in chemical reactions rather than functioning only as an inert container.</p>\n<p>Question 13 clearly includes established functions such as waste excretion, temperature regulation, and the reaction medium. The difficulty lies in its phrases <strong>energy transformation</strong> and <strong>energy metabolism regulation</strong>. Water is not a caloric fuel, but it is involved in biochemical processes related to metabolism. Therefore, “water supplies no Calories” does not logically prove that water has no role in energy transformations.</p>\n<p>Learn the clear functions and preserve this distinction: <strong>not fuel is not the same as not involved</strong>. Without the course&#x27;s explanation or official key, the intended choice between those vague phrases cannot be certified. This is a wording problem worth clarifying, not evidence that you failed to understand water.</p>"},{"id":"l1-12","title":"11 Phytochemicals antioxidants and scientific caution","html":"<p><strong>Phytochemicals</strong> are plant-produced compounds. The prefix phyto means plant, which resolves the main classification in question 11. Some have biological effects of interest to health, but the category is broad: being natural or plant-derived does not guarantee benefit, safety, or a need for supplementation.</p>\n<p>Oxidation is a normal part of chemistry and metabolism. Reactive molecules can participate in ordinary cell processes, but excessive oxidative damage relative to protective systems is described as oxidative stress. Antioxidants can help limit particular oxidation reactions. This is not a simple battle in which every reactive molecule is harmful and every antioxidant dose is helpful.</p>\n<p>Vitamins C and E have antioxidant functions. Beta-carotene is a carotenoid and a <strong>provitamin A compound</strong>, meaning the body can convert it to vitamin A. It is often discussed with antioxidant compounds, but it is not itself a vitamin named beta-carotene. This creates the wording issue in question 12: a broad list of antioxidant nutrients differs from a strict list of vitamins.</p>\n<p>Do not jump from laboratory antioxidant activity to a promise that a supplement prevents disease. Clinical evidence does not support that general leap, and some high-dose antioxidant supplements have caused harm in particular populations. <a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/antioxidant-supplements-what-you-need-to-know\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCCIH antioxidant evidence</a></p>\n<p>Question 19 lists several groups of plant foods. Oranges, peppers, leafy greens, berries, and sweet potatoes offer different combinations of vitamins and phytochemicals. Bananas, raisins, and figs are not devoid of antioxidant compounds either. “Good source of antioxidants” lacks a single standardized threshold across this whole category. Do not confidently exclude the first group without knowing which nutrient or course definition is intended. Variety is a more useful real-world principle than dividing these fruits into antioxidant foods and non-antioxidant foods.</p>"},{"id":"l1-13","title":"12 Nutrient density and energy density","html":"<p><strong>Nutrient density</strong> compares useful nutrient content with the energy provided. <strong>Energy density</strong> describes Calories relative to the amount or weight of food. These are related ideas, not opposites.</p>\n<p>Compare a drink supplying mostly sugar with a food supplying protein, fibre, and several micronutrients for similar energy. The latter may offer more nutritional value per Calorie. But a calorie-rich food can still be nutritious: nuts and seeds contain fats and energy alongside useful nutrients. A low-calorie product with almost no nutrients is not automatically nutrient dense.</p>\n<p>Question 14&#x27;s wording combines good nutrient content with low Calories. That is a common introductory shortcut, but the more durable definition is <strong>nutrients relative to energy</strong>. Do not memorize that all high-calorie foods are nutrient poor or all animal foods are nutrient dense.</p>\n<p>For counselling, density is descriptive, not a moral judgment. Someone with limited appetite or increased needs may benefit from foods that provide both energy and nutrients. The goal depends on the person&#x27;s circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"l1-14","title":"13 Nutrition assessment using ABCD","html":"<p>An assessment assembles evidence; it does not diagnose nutritional status from one number. The mnemonic <strong>ABCD</strong> helps organize four traditional components.</p>\n<p><strong>A is anthropometric:</strong> body measurements such as height, weight, growth patterns, and changes over time. A single weight lacks context. In children and teenagers, growth and development matter; adult interpretations should not simply be transferred.</p>\n<p><strong>B is biochemical:</strong> laboratory measures relevant to a question about nutritional status. A result can be influenced by inflammation, hydration, illness, or other factors. A low or high value needs interpretation, not automatic attribution to the last food someone ate.</p>\n<p><strong>C is clinical:</strong> medical history, symptoms, physical findings, medications, and relevant functional information. Many symptoms have multiple possible causes. Fatigue alone does not prove iron deficiency, for example.</p>\n<p><strong>D is dietary:</strong> information about foods, drinks, supplements, patterns, access, and intake. A 24-hour recall can be useful but may not represent usual intake. Records can be incomplete, and people may change eating while recording it.</p>\n<p>For question 15, “initial evaluation” is a general description of a process, not one of these four named components. <strong>Memory aid: measure, test, examine, ask</strong> corresponds to anthropometric, biochemical, clinical, and dietary.</p>\n<p>The components should inform one another. A person reporting little food access, showing an unintentional weight change, and having relevant clinical findings needs a broader assessment than a nutrient calculation alone. This framework supports respectful enquiry rather than assumptions based on appearance.</p>"},{"id":"l1-15","title":"14 Primary and secondary malnutrition","html":"<p>Malnutrition broadly includes insufficient, excessive, or imbalanced nutrition; in the primary-versus-secondary distinction, courses often focus on undernutrition and deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary malnutrition</strong> arises from insufficient intake. Not having enough money for food can directly restrict what is available, making it a potential primary cause as in question 24. Food insecurity is not a failure of willpower or knowledge. Education alone cannot solve lack of resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary malnutrition</strong> occurs when illness or another condition interferes with digestion, absorption, utilization, or nutrient losses and needs. Someone may have access to enough food yet fail to absorb a nutrient adequately. Primary and secondary factors can coexist: illness might impair absorption while also reducing appetite and income.</p>\n<p>The practical reasoning is to ask where the difficulty occurs: <strong>getting enough in</strong>, or <strong>being able to process and use it</strong>, or both. This distinction guides what further information and support are needed.</p>"},{"id":"l1-16","title":"15 Supplement claims and the country distinction","html":"<p>Question 20 appears to test the US distinction between supporting normal body structure or function and claiming to prevent or treat disease. A statement about supporting normal bones differs from a claim that a product prevents fractures. Wording, context, evidence, and the applicable regulatory category all matter.</p>\n<p>The word “approved” in the question is misleading: ordinary US dietary-supplement structure/function claims are not individually preapproved by FDA. Manufacturers must meet applicable requirements, including substantiation and required labelling. A plausible-sounding sentence does not establish that a particular product is effective or legally compliant. <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/food/nutrition-food-labeling-and-critical-foods/structurefunction-claims\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA structure and function claims</a></p>\n<p>For this test, identify the contrast it appears to seek, but do not label every other statement automatically approved. In Canada, natural health products use a different licensing framework, and authorized products carry identifiers such as an NPN. A Canadian product should be checked against its licensed information rather than evaluated solely using the US textbook shortcut. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/natural-non-prescription/applications-submissions/product-licensing/licensed-natural-health-products-database.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada licensed natural health products database</a></p>"},{"id":"l1-17","title":"16 A practical study method for this test","html":"<p>Make a one-page recall sheet from memory with four blocks: nutrient classes and sugar pairs; DRI definitions; 4–4–9 and the percentage formula; and ADEK, B/C, ABCD. Then compare it with this guide and correct omissions in a different colour.</p>\n<p>Redo questions 6, 7, 8, and 16–18 on blank paper. Write units at every step. If the number is wrong, decide whether the error came from the factor, serving fraction, denominator, or double-counting. That diagnosis is more useful than repeating the same calculation mechanically.</p>\n<p>Explain five distinctions aloud: EAR versus RDA; DV versus AMDR; grams versus energy percentage; nutrient density versus energy density; and primary versus secondary malnutrition. If you can supply your own example of each, you are moving beyond memorization.</p>\n<p>Keep a short clarification list for the instructor: the label era and jurisdiction in questions 3–5; the wording of vitamins versus carotenoids in 12; water&#x27;s vague energy terms in 13; the intended density definition in 14; the threshold for antioxidant sources in 19; “approved” claims in 20; and the intended generalizations in 22–23. Ask for the course interpretation without abandoning the more accurate concepts.</p>"},{"id":"l1-18","title":"Practice questions","html":"<p>These are original practice items, not an official answer key. Answer without looking at the next section.</p>\n<ol><li>A reference intake meets the needs of about half a healthy life-stage group. Name it and explain why it is not the same as an RDA.</li><li>Name the two sugars in sucrose. Which disaccharide contains glucose twice?</li><li>A snack contains 30 g carbohydrate, 6 g protein, and 8 g fat per package. Calculate total kcal and the percentage of energy from fat.</li><li>A recipe contains 80 g carbohydrate, 20 g protein, and 12 g fat and makes four equal portions. How much energy is in one portion?</li><li>A day&#x27;s intake contains 70 g total fat, including 18 g saturated fat. How many fat Calories should enter the total energy calculation? Explain the trap.</li><li>A food supplies 15 percent DV for a nutrient. Does that mean 15 percent of the food&#x27;s energy comes from that nutrient? Explain.</li><li>A 16-year-old obtains 24 percent of dietary energy from fat. Compare that with the age-appropriate AMDR, without diagnosing the person.</li><li>Name the fat-soluble vitamins and the water-soluble groups. Give one reason the phrase “water-soluble vitamins cannot accumulate or cause harm” is unreliable.</li><li>Explain why beans eaten with a vitamin C-rich food can be nutritionally useful. Does this mean that all minerals are absorbed better than all vitamins?</li><li>A person cannot afford enough food. Another eats adequately but has an illness that impairs absorption. Identify the primary-versus-secondary distinction and one limitation of this simplified classification.</li><li>Place height measurement, a relevant blood test, a physical symptom review, and a food record into ABCD. Why should one result not determine the whole assessment?</li><li>Explain two problems with the claim “every food or supplement advertised as antioxidant prevents disease.” Then explain why a historical question about mandatory vitamin C on labels needs context.</li></ol>"},{"id":"l1-19","title":"Practice answers and explanations","html":"<ol><li><strong>EAR.</strong> It is the midpoint-type requirement reference for the specified group, whereas the RDA is intended to cover nearly all. Half the people is not the same as half one person&#x27;s requirement.</li><li><strong>Sucrose is glucose plus fructose; maltose is glucose plus glucose.</strong> Lactose is glucose plus galactose. Distinguish sugar units from the disaccharides built from them.</li><li><strong>216 kcal; about 33.3 percent from fat.</strong> Carbohydrate contributes 120, protein 24, and fat 72 kcal. Total 216; 72 ÷ 216 × 100 = 33.3. Dividing 8 fat grams by 216 kcal would mix units.</li><li><strong>127 kcal per portion.</strong> The full recipe supplies 320 + 80 + 108 = 508 kcal; 508 ÷ 4 = 127. Always check whether the supplied quantities describe the whole recipe or a serving.</li><li><strong>630 kcal.</strong> Multiply 70 × 9. The 18 g saturated fat is already included, so adding 18 × 9 again would overcount.</li><li><strong>No.</strong> Percent DV compares a nutrient amount with a label reference; percent energy compares energy from a nutrient with the food&#x27;s total energy. Minerals can have a percent DV while supplying no Calories.</li><li><strong>24 percent is below the 25–35 percent range for ages 4–18.</strong> The comparison alone does not diagnose inadequacy; usual intake, needs, and the broader assessment matter. Using the adult 20–35 range would miss the age distinction.</li><li><strong>ADEK are fat soluble; B vitamins and C are water soluble.</strong> B12 has substantial stores, and excessive supplemental B6 can cause harm. Solubility is useful but not a complete safety or deficiency-timing rule.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C can increase nonheme iron absorption.</strong> Beans contain nonheme iron. This specific interaction does not establish any universal absorption ranking between entire nutrient classes.</li><li><strong>Insufficient food intake is a primary pathway; impaired absorption is secondary.</strong> They can coexist, and an actual assessment must identify causes and practical supports rather than simply assign a label.</li><li><strong>Height is anthropometric; blood test biochemical; symptoms clinical; food record dietary.</strong> Each gives only part of the picture and may have limitations or confounders. Agreement across evidence is more informative than one isolated result.</li><li><strong>Biological antioxidant activity is not proof of disease prevention, and supplement dose and context can change risks.</strong> Food patterns and isolated supplements are not interchangeable. Vitamin C was part of the older US mandatory panel but is generally optional in the current format; the test&#x27;s label must be interpreted in its historical setting.</li></ol>"},{"id":"l1-20","title":"Complete test coverage map","html":"<p>This map shows where to study and how confidently to interpret the question. It is not an official answer key.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Test question</th><th scope=\"col\">Main topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Study section</th><th scope=\"col\">Important caution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>EAR versus other reference values</td><td>2</td><td>Half a specified healthy group</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Sucrose building blocks</td><td>3</td><td>Glucose plus fructose</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Food labels and daily references</td><td>6</td><td>Wording and label jurisdiction need clarification</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Enriched grain nutrients</td><td>7</td><td>Universal MUST is too broad</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Missing nutrient on legacy label</td><td>6</td><td>Older US vitamin C convention, not current rule</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Fat energy percentage</td><td>4 and 5</td><td>45 divided by 170; not percent DV</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Smoothie total energy</td><td>4</td><td>Use 4–4–9 separately</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Half sandwich energy</td><td>4</td><td>Adjust whole-food amounts for half eaten</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Milk and calcium</td><td>7 and 9</td><td>Milk supplies multiple nutrients</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Sodium and blood pressure</td><td>9</td><td>Recognize processed-food sources</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Phytochemicals</td><td>11</td><td>Plant-produced does not guarantee benefit</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Antioxidant vitamins and beta-carotene</td><td>11</td><td>Vitamin wording differs from broad antioxidant list</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Water roles</td><td>10</td><td>Energy terms are ambiguous</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Nutrient density</td><td>12</td><td>Relative to energy, not simply low calorie</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>ABCD assessment</td><td>13</td><td>Initial evaluation is not a named ABCD component</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Jill carbohydrate energy</td><td>4</td><td>250 multiplied by 4</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Jill carbohydrate percentage</td><td>4 and 5</td><td>Divide by 2100 kcal</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Jill fat percentage</td><td>4 and 5</td><td>Saturated fat is included in total fat</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Antioxidant food sources</td><td>11</td><td>No single threshold justifies excluding first group</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Supplement claim types</td><td>15</td><td>Approved is misleading; country matters</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Water-soluble vitamins</td><td>8</td><td>B vitamins and C</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Storage and deficiency timing</td><td>8</td><td>General pattern has exceptions including B12</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Mineral versus vitamin absorption</td><td>9</td><td>No universal group ranking</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Primary malnutrition</td><td>14</td><td>Food affordability is a potential intake barrier</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Comparative iron sources</td><td>9</td><td>Compare listed servings; peaches contribute less</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"l1-21","title":"Readiness checklist","html":"<p>You are ready to attempt the test when you can explain all the key distinctions without reading, calculate the energy examples correctly twice on different attempts, interpret Jill&#x27;s intake without double-counting saturated fat, and identify which questions need historical or wording context. Being prepared includes recognizing uncertainty rather than confidently learning a misleading rule.</p>\n<p>This guide is educational study support, not individualized nutrition treatment. Its sources were checked on 27 October 2026. Reconcile flagged questions with the course materials or instructor before treating any suggested interpretation as the course&#x27;s official answer.</p>"}]},"lesson-2":{"title":"Preconception Nutrition","subtitle":"Understand the science, practise the reasoning, and prepare for your 20-question lesson test.","source":"Test-aligned supplementary study companion. Your supplied NC7 Lesson Test 2a contains 20 questions and no official answer key. The complete assigned textbook chapter has not been supplied, so this is not a verified chapter summary. Linked current primary sources support the explanations; original examples and practice are study aids, not an official answer key.","sections":[{"title":"A deep study guide: understand the biology, apply the numbers, practise the reasoning","html":"<p><strong>Study status:</strong> This is an independently written, source-based companion for your course’s Lesson 2, not a verified summary of the assigned textbook chapter. Your supplied 20-question Lesson 2 test is available and mapped below; the complete assigned chapter and official answer key were not supplied. Separate practice questions are original, not an official answer key. Canadian guidance is prioritized; international clinical sources are identified. Checked October 27, 2026.</p>","id":"l2-1"},{"title":"What you need to be able to explain","html":"<p>By the end, aim to do more than recognize a supplement name. You should be able to:</p>\n<ol><li>Explain why preparation matters before someone knows they are pregnant.</li><li>Connect reproductive function with adequate energy, nutrient supply and medical care without claiming that food controls fertility.</li><li>Distinguish folate, folic acid and dietary folate equivalents; calculate unit conversions correctly.</li><li>Separate a nutrient’s recommended <strong>total intake</strong> from a recommended <strong>supplement dose</strong>.</li><li>Identify food and supplement issues that deserve assessment, not an immediate diagnosis.</li><li>Discuss pregnancy preparation without weight stigma, blame or unrealistic food rules.</li><li>Choose safe, achievable counselling steps and recognize when another professional is needed.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Central idea:</strong> Preconception nutrition is preparation, not a promise. It can address avoidable gaps and support health. It cannot guarantee conception, prevent every complication or explain every pregnancy loss. A person who experiences infertility has not necessarily eaten incorrectly.</p>\n<h4>Three outcomes that must not be confused</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Nutrient adequacy:</strong> obtaining and using enough of a nutrient.</li><li><strong>Reproductive function:</strong> processes such as hormone signalling, ovulation and sperm production.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy outcome:</strong> whether conception occurs and a pregnancy progresses to a healthy birth.</li></ul>\n<p>Improvement in the first does not automatically prove improvement in the third. For example, a product can raise a blood nutrient concentration without evidence that it increases live births. When assessing a claim, ask: <em>What was actually measured? In whom? Compared with what?</em></p>","id":"l2-2"},{"title":"The reproductive timeline: why “before” matters","html":"<h4>The basic sequence</h4>\n<p>In a typical ovulatory cycle, communication between the brain, pituitary gland and ovaries coordinates follicle development, ovulation and changes in the uterine lining. Follicle-stimulating hormone supports follicular development; luteinizing hormone is involved in triggering ovulation. Estrogen and progesterone help coordinate the cycle and prepare the endometrium. These are interacting feedback systems, not independent switches.</p>\n<p>The fertile window is commonly described as the six-day interval ending on ovulation. This reflects the survival of sperm before an egg is released; it does not mean everyone ovulates on cycle day 14. Calendar predictions are estimates, especially when cycles vary. Nutrition counselling is not a substitute for fertility assessment. <a href=\"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/optimizing-natural-fertility-a-committee-opinion-2021/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASRM: optimizing natural fertility</a></p>\n<p>Fertilization usually occurs in a fallopian tube. The resulting cells divide as they move toward the uterus. A blastocyst subsequently implants in the uterine lining; fertilization and implantation are different events. Early cells contribute to the embryo and supporting structures, including the placenta. The placenta develops progressively rather than becoming a completely formed nutrient-delivery organ at the instant of fertilization. <a href=\"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/how-your-fetus-grows-during-pregnancy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ACOG: fetal development</a>; <a href=\"https://www.ucsfhealth.org/health-articles/conception-how-it-works\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">UCSF: conception</a></p>\n<h4>Two clocks used in pregnancy</h4>\n<p><strong>Gestational age</strong> is usually counted from the first day of the last menstrual period. <strong>Time since conception</strong> starts later. In a simplified 28-day-cycle example, fertilization at approximately two weeks after that period means “six weeks pregnant” is approximately four weeks after fertilization. Real cycles and clinical dating vary.</p>\n<p>Why does this matter? A statement such as “development begins in week three” is ambiguous unless you know which clock the writer uses. In an assessment, read the wording before choosing an answer. A calculation based on the wrong starting point can be wrong even when the arithmetic is perfect.</p>\n<h4>The neural tube and the preparation window</h4>\n<p>The neural tube forms the early basis of the brain and spinal cord. Its closure happens very early, within about 28 days after conception, often before pregnancy is recognized. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Pregnancy-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: pregnancy</a> This is why folic acid guidance starts before conception rather than waiting for a first prenatal appointment. Adequate folic acid reduces the risk of folate-sensitive neural tube defects; it does not eliminate all birth-defect risk. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/services/pregnancy/folic-acid.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PHAC: folic acid and neural tube defects</a></p>\n<p><strong>Original timeline exercise:</strong> Imagine someone starts planning in January, conceives in April and notices a missed period later that month. A plan begun in January can establish a supplement routine, identify food-access barriers and arrange a medication review. Waiting until May misses part of that preparation opportunity. If pregnancy has already begun, the response is to start appropriate care now—not suggest that the opportunity for a healthy pregnancy has disappeared.</p>","id":"l2-3"},{"title":"Nutritional status is more than what someone ate yesterday","html":"<p>Think of nutritional status as the result of several processes:</p>\n<p><strong>Availability → intake → digestion and absorption → use and losses → body stores and function.</strong></p>\n<p>Two people reporting similar menus may have different needs because one has heavy menstrual bleeding, malabsorption, a restrictive eating pattern or a medical condition. Conversely, one low-intake day does not establish a chronic deficiency. Assessment needs context and, when indicated, clinical investigation.</p>\n<p>Separate these concepts:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th><th scope=\"col\">What it does not prove</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intake</td><td>What is eaten or taken as a supplement</td><td>How much is absorbed</td></tr><tr><td>Bioavailability</td><td>How available a nutrient is for absorption/use</td><td>That every person absorbs the same amount</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrient stores</td><td>The body’s reserves</td><td>That a short food record reveals them</td></tr><tr><td>Deficiency</td><td>Insufficient nutrient status affecting health or function</td><td>That every nonspecific symptom has a nutritional cause</td></tr><tr><td>Adequacy</td><td>Enough for the relevant needs</td><td>That more will improve fertility</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A useful exam habit is to distinguish an <strong>observation</strong> from an <strong>inference</strong>. “The client reports no fortified foods or B12 supplements” is an observation. “The client definitely has B12 deficiency” is an unsupported diagnostic inference without further assessment.</p>\n<h4>Energy availability and reproductive function</h4>\n<p>Energy availability refers to dietary energy remaining after exercise expenditure for other physiological functions. A person can eat vegetables, lean protein and whole grains yet still not eat enough overall. Chronic under-fuelling can contribute to disruption of reproductive hormone signalling. In functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, care includes addressing energy imbalance, often through changes in food intake, activity and psychological support. The diagnosis requires exclusion of other causes. <a href=\"https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/hypothalamic-amenorrhea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Endocrine Society: hypothalamic amenorrhea guideline</a></p>\n<p>Do not diagnose low energy availability from body size, athletic identity or a single missed period. Ask about eating regularity, training changes, unintentional restriction, fatigue, menstrual changes and stress. Missed periods deserve medical assessment; pregnancy, thyroid disorders, PCOS and other conditions may need consideration. <a href=\"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/current-evaluation-of-amenorrhea/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASRM: evaluation of amenorrhea</a></p>\n<p><strong>Worked reasoning:</strong> A runner eats a small salad at lunch and trains after work. The first task is not to calculate an arbitrary “fertility calorie target.” Ask whether the lunch sustains them, what they eat before and after training, whether their cycle has changed and whether food rules make eating difficult. The initial nutritional problem may be meal adequacy, while the menstrual change needs parallel clinical review.</p>\n<h4>Weight-neutral does not mean risk-blind</h4>\n<p>Ask permission before discussing weight. A weight measure may contribute to a broader clinical assessment, but it cannot reveal dietary quality, nutrient stores, eating-disorder history or how someone should be treated. Discuss relevant health markers and behaviours without treating weight change as the sole definition of success. Avoid promising pregnancy after weight loss or prescribing crash diets before conception. A respectful plan might prioritize regular meals, medication review, blood-pressure follow-up and access to care while an appropriate clinician discusses individual risks.</p>","id":"l2-4"},{"title":"Folate: the highest-priority distinction to master","html":"<h4>Three terms, three meanings</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Folate</strong> is the general term for vitamin B9 forms, including those naturally present in foods.</li><li><strong>Folic acid</strong> is a form used in many supplements and fortified foods.</li><li><strong>Dietary folate equivalents (DFE)</strong> account for differences in bioavailability. They are not simply another spelling of “micrograms of folic acid.”</li></ul>\n<p>For conversion purposes, <strong>1 mcg DFE = 1 mcg naturally occurring food folate = 0.6 mcg folic acid consumed with food</strong>. Adult folate RDA is <strong>400 mcg DFE/day</strong>; in pregnancy it is <strong>600 mcg DFE/day</strong>. These are total-intake reference values. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Folate-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: folate</a></p>\n<h4>Canadian prevention advice</h4>\n<p>For anyone who could become pregnant, Canadian guidance recommends a daily multivitamin containing <strong>400 mcg (0.4 mg) folic acid</strong>, ideally started <strong>at least three months before pregnancy</strong>. Folate-containing foods remain important; they do not replace this prevention recommendation. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/services/pregnancy/folic-acid.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PHAC: folic acid</a></p>\n<p>Some higher-risk situations require individualized assessment. A previous neural-tube-defect-affected pregnancy, relevant personal/family history, certain medications or medical conditions should prompt a clinician/pharmacist discussion rather than a counsellor selecting a higher dose. Do not double a multivitamin to obtain more folic acid: every other ingredient increases too. Professional consultation is advised about doses above <strong>1 mg/day</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/public-health/services/pregnancy/folic-acid.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PHAC: folic acid</a> Higher-risk guidance: <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-guide/explore/dietary-guidelines/applying/advice-vitamin-mineral-supplementation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: vitamin and mineral supplementation</a></p>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — milligrams to micrograms</h4>\n<p>Because <strong>1 mg = 1,000 mcg</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>0.4 mg × 1,000 = 400 mcg.</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, “0.4 mg folic acid” and “400 mcg folic acid” express the same amount. A label showing 4 mg is not equivalent: it is 4,000 mcg, ten times 0.4 mg. The calculation identifies a difference; it does not authorize a dose change.</p>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — DFE is not a supplement instruction</h4>\n<p>Conversion convention also documented in <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Pregnancy-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS’s pregnancy fact sheet</a>. The numbers below are an original teaching example.</p>\n<p>An invented menu supplies 250 mcg naturally occurring folate. A supplement supplies 400 mcg folic acid taken with food.</p>\n<ul><li>Food contribution: 250 mcg DFE.</li><li>Supplement contribution: 400 ÷ 0.6 ≈ 667 mcg DFE.</li><li>Combined: approximately <strong>917 mcg DFE</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>This does <strong>not</strong> mean the person took 917 mcg folic acid. They took 400 mcg folic acid plus food folate. Nor should they reduce the routinely recommended folic acid simply because the DFE calculation exceeds an RDA. Prevention guidance and total-intake accounting answer different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Test trap:</strong> An RDA is not a toxicity threshold. Likewise, a supplement prevention recommendation is not a daily total from every possible source. Read both the nutrient form and the unit.</p>","id":"l2-5"},{"title":"The nutrient map: learn role, source, number and limitation","html":"<h4>Reference values worth understanding</h4>\n<p>The following values apply to <strong>females aged 19–50</strong>, before pregnancy and during pregnancy. They are <strong>daily total-intake reference values</strong>, not individualized prescriptions or automatic tablet doses. Choline uses an Adequate Intake (AI); the other values shown are RDAs.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Nutrient</th><th scope=\"col\">Nonpregnant</th><th scope=\"col\">Pregnant</th><th scope=\"col\">Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Folate</td><td>400</td><td>600</td><td>mcg DFE</td></tr><tr><td>Iron</td><td>18</td><td>27</td><td>mg</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamin B12</td><td>2.4</td><td>2.6</td><td>mcg</td></tr><tr><td>Iodine</td><td>150</td><td>220</td><td>mcg</td></tr><tr><td>Choline, AI</td><td>425</td><td>450</td><td>mg</td></tr><tr><td>Calcium</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,000</td><td>mg</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamin D</td><td>15 / 600</td><td>15 / 600</td><td>mcg / IU</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Calcium differs for ages <strong>14–18</strong>: <strong>1,300 mg/day</strong>, including pregnancy. Do not apply the adult row to every adolescent. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-elements.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: mineral reference values</a>; <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/healthy-eating/dietary-reference-intakes/tables/reference-values-vitamins.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: vitamin reference values</a></p>\n<p><strong>RDA:</strong> intake intended to meet the needs of nearly all healthy people in the specified group. <strong>AI:</strong> used when evidence is insufficient to establish an RDA; it is not evidence that the nutrient is unimportant. Neither number diagnoses an individual’s status from one day of food intake.</p>\n<h4>Iron: oxygen transport and reserves</h4>\n<p>Iron is part of haemoglobin and supports oxygen transport. Heme iron occurs in animal foods; plant foods and fortified foods supply nonheme iron. Beans, lentils, tofu and fortified cereals can contribute. Vitamin-C-containing foods can improve absorption of nonheme iron. Heavy bleeding increases concern about iron depletion. Fatigue has many possible causes, so assessment may include clinician-directed bloodwork rather than an immediate iron prescription. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: iron</a></p>\n<p>Pregnancy has its own Canadian supplement advice: a daily multivitamin containing <strong>16–20 mg iron</strong> and <strong>0.4 mg folic acid</strong>. The 16–20 mg supplement recommendation is not the same thing as the pregnancy <strong>27 mg total-intake RDA</strong>, and it is not a universal preconception treatment dose. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-guide/explore/dietary-guidelines/applying/advice-nutrition-different-life-stages/considerations-pregnancy-support-breastfeeding.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: pregnancy nutrition</a></p>\n<h4>Vitamin B12: reliable supply matters</h4>\n<p>B12 supports neurological function and red blood cell formation. Animal foods and appropriately fortified foods provide it; a vegan pattern needs a dependable fortified-food or supplement strategy. Seaweed or unverified fermented foods should not be assumed to supply reliable B12. Absorption can be affected by some medical conditions and medicines. Correcting folate intake does not correct B12 deficiency or protect against all its neurological consequences. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminB12-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: vitamin B12</a></p>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong> “Plant-based” is not a diagnosis of deficiency. Ask which products are fortified, their labelled amounts and how consistently they are consumed. If someone buys a fortified beverage but only drinks it once a month, its presence in the refrigerator is not a reliable daily plan.</p>\n<h4>Iodine: thyroid hormone production</h4>\n<p>Iodine is needed to make thyroid hormones, which support metabolism and early neurological development. Sources can include dairy, seafood and iodized salt; amounts vary. Specialty salts are not necessarily iodized. Seaweed iodine content can be very high and variable, making kelp supplements a poor casual substitute for an individualized plan. Both inadequate and excessive iodine can cause problems. Thyroid disease warrants clinician involvement, not self-directed iodine treatment. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iodine-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: iodine</a></p>\n<h4>Choline: membranes and signalling</h4>\n<p>Choline contributes to cell membranes, acetylcholine production and other metabolic processes. Eggs, meat, fish, soy foods and some legumes contribute. Many multivitamins contain little or none, so “I take a prenatal” does not establish adequate intake of every nutrient. Its reference value is an <strong>AI</strong>, not an RDA. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Choline-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: choline</a></p>\n<h4>Calcium and vitamin D: separate but connected</h4>\n<p>Calcium supports bones and other physiological functions; vitamin D helps the body use calcium. Food planning can include dairy foods or appropriately fortified alternatives, calcium-set tofu and other suitable foods. Check labels rather than assuming all plant beverages have equivalent nutrients. Calcium and vitamin D are related, but one does not replace the other.</p>\n<p>Canadian practical vitamin D guidance for ages <strong>2–50</strong> is to eat vitamin-D-containing foods daily or, if not doing so, take a <strong>400 IU (10 mcg) supplement</strong>. This public-health advice is distinct from the <strong>600 IU total-intake RDA</strong> for adults in the table. Existing multivitamin content matters when considering an additional product. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/nutrients/vitamin-d.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: vitamin D</a></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 3 — interpret a hypothetical label:</strong> A product contains 10 mcg vitamin D. Since 1 mcg equals 40 IU, it provides <strong>400 IU</strong>. The label amount does not prove that the person’s total intake is only 400 IU: food contributes too. It also does not justify adding multiple products without reviewing the whole plan.</p>","id":"l2-6"},{"title":"Build a feasible pattern, not a “fertility diet”","html":"<p>Use foods the person enjoys and can obtain. A practical meal-building approach is to combine a satisfying carbohydrate food, a protein food, vegetables or fruit, and suitable fats. This is a flexible planning aid, not a rule that every meal must look identical. Cultural staples such as rice, beans, tortillas, lentils, noodles, fish, yogurt or stews can fit without being replaced by expensive marketed alternatives.</p>\n<p>Food security changes what advice is useful. Ask whether food runs out, whether there is cooking equipment, whether shifts interrupt meals and whether nausea or gastrointestinal symptoms limit choices. Frozen vegetables, canned beans, eggs, oats and batch-cooked meals may be useful options where affordable and acceptable. “Buy organic fertility superfoods” is not a meaningful solution to an empty pantry. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-guide/eating-support/meal-planning-grocery-shopping/healthy-eating-budget.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: healthy eating on a budget</a></p>\n<p><strong>Original meal-planning exercise:</strong> A client regularly eats toast and coffee, skips lunch and has rice and chicken at dinner. Rather than remove the rice, investigate the missing midday meal. Possibilities include a bean-and-rice portion saved from dinner, a sandwich plus fruit, or yogurt/fortified soy beverage with oats and another food that makes the meal satisfying. The best option depends on cost, refrigeration, preference and appetite. Ask what they would actually repeat.</p>\n<h4>Fish: balance nutrients with mercury advice</h4>\n<p>Do not translate mercury advice into “avoid all fish.” Canadian advice for people who are or may become pregnant limits the combined intake of <strong>fresh/frozen tuna, shark, swordfish, marlin, orange roughy and escolar to 150 g per month</strong>. Canned albacore/white tuna has a separate <strong>300 g per week</strong> limit. Canned light tuna is not the same category. Salmon and other lower-mercury options can provide variety; locally caught fish may have regional advisories. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/food-safety/chemical-contaminants/environmental-contaminants/mercury/mercury-fish.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: mercury in fish</a></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 4:</strong> Two 90 g portions of the listed high-mercury fish in one month total <strong>180 g</strong>, above the 150 g/month recommendation. The period is a <strong>month</strong>, not a week, and the limit applies to the listed fish <strong>combined</strong>, not 150 g of each. A 90 g portion of salmon is not added to that specific high-mercury total.</p>","id":"l2-7"},{"title":"Medical and supplement review: know your boundaries","html":"<h4>Diabetes</h4>\n<p>Established type 1 or type 2 diabetes requires a clinician-led preconception plan. Glucose management before and early in pregnancy matters; medicines and relevant complications may need review. Nutrition care supports this plan, but a prenatal multivitamin does not replace it. Gestational diabetes is a pregnancy-related category distinguished clinically from pre-existing or overt diabetes; it is not simply another name for type 1 or type 2 diabetes. Someone with a history of gestational diabetes also benefits from appropriate follow-up and planning. <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/diabetes-pregnancy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIDDK: pregnancy with diabetes</a></p>\n<h4>PCOS</h4>\n<p>PCOS may involve irregular ovulation and metabolic concerns, but not every person has the same symptoms or needs. The international guideline does not identify one dietary composition as superior for all relevant PCOS outcomes. Sustainable, individualized eating and activity plans are preferable to declaring that everyone must avoid carbohydrates. Weight stigma and disordered eating risk deserve attention. Nutrition support does not replace evaluation or evidence-based fertility treatment where needed. <a href=\"https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/3379521/Evidence-Based-Guidelines-2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2023 international PCOS guideline</a></p>\n<h4>Medicines, herbs and overlapping products</h4>\n<p>Ask for an actual list or photographs: prescriptions, over-the-counter medicines, multivitamins, single nutrients, powders, teas and “fertility” blends. Include dose and frequency. Arrange prescriber/pharmacist review before pregnancy; do not independently stop essential treatment. The risks of an untreated condition also matter. <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/pregnancy/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CDC: planning for pregnancy</a></p>\n<p>Excessive <strong>preformed vitamin A</strong> can cause fetal harm. This is not the same issue as eating beta-carotene-rich vegetables. “Natural” is not a safety guarantee, and a product containing ten ingredients may duplicate a multivitamin. Review form, amount and cumulative exposure rather than judging by packaging. <a href=\"https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminA-HealthProfessional/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIH ODS: vitamin A</a></p>\n<p><strong>Original product audit:</strong> One multivitamin provides nutrient X, a hair supplement also provides X and a drink powder adds X again. Before asking “Which brand is best?”, add the labelled daily amounts and identify whether each is actually needed. The same logic applies to duplicate folic acid, vitamin D and iron. The audit is an information-gathering step, not permission to prescribe a replacement regimen.</p>","id":"l2-8"},{"title":"Alcohol, caffeine, smoking and partner factors","html":"<p>Avoid alcohol when trying to conceive and during pregnancy; no safe pregnancy amount or timing has been established. Support smoking cessation and avoidance of cannabis in pregnancy, including discussion of exposure from others. Someone who used a substance before recognizing pregnancy needs compassionate clinical advice, not a prediction that harm definitely occurred. Dependence or difficulty stopping warrants professional support; do not abruptly stop prescribed medicines or substance-use treatment without medical guidance. <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/maternal-infant-health/pregnancy-substance-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CDC: substance use during pregnancy</a></p>\n<p>Health Canada recommends a maximum <strong>300 mg caffeine/day</strong> for people planning pregnancy and during pregnancy. Count all sources, not only coffee. Cup sizes, brewing methods and products differ; 300 mg is a ceiling, not a target. <a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-nutrition/food-safety/food-additives/caffeine-foods.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Health Canada: caffeine</a></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example 5:</strong> Using invented labelled amounts, coffee 180 mg + tea 45 mg + chocolate 15 mg = <strong>240 mg</strong>. Another identical coffee raises the day to <strong>420 mg</strong>. Replacing that second coffee with an option containing 5 mg would make <strong>245 mg</strong>. These numbers teach addition; actual products must be checked.</p>\n<p>Include sperm-producing partners where relevant and with the client’s permission. Discuss general health, smoking/substance exposure and appropriate medical assessment without assigning all responsibility to one partner. Antioxidant or vitamin “male fertility” supplements have uncertain clinical usefulness; evidence for a semen-marker change is not automatically evidence for pregnancy or live birth benefit. <a href=\"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/diagnosis-and-treatment-of-infertility-in-men-aua-asrm-guideline-part2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AUA/ASRM: male infertility guideline</a></p>","id":"l2-9"},{"title":"A counselling structure you can actually use","html":"<p><strong>READY</strong> is an original memory aid for this guide, not a validated clinical assessment tool.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>R — Reproductive plans:</strong> Ask whether pregnancy is possible or desired, the intended timing, and what the person wants help with. Do not assume everyone wants children.</li><li><strong>E — Eating and energy:</strong> Explore usual meals, food access, cultural preferences, dietary exclusions, activity, menstrual concerns and symptoms.</li><li><strong>A — Assess supplements:</strong> Record product, nutrient form, dose, frequency and duplicate ingredients. Check routine folic acid guidance.</li><li><strong>D — Discuss coordination:</strong> Identify medical, medication, bleeding, malabsorption, eating-disorder or fertility concerns needing another professional.</li><li><strong>Y — Your next achievable step:</strong> Agree on one or two actions, how success will be noticed and when to review.</li></ul>\n<p>Use teach-back: “Just so I know I explained it clearly, what will you look for on the folic acid label?” This checks the explanation without turning the conversation into a test of the client.</p>\n<p>Document facts separately from interpretation. “Client reports heavy periods and fatigue; clinician review recommended” is more accurate than “iron-deficient because vegan.” Record the agreed action, any referral and the client’s priorities. A useful follow-up asks what happened, what was difficult and what needs adapting—not whether the person was “good.”</p>\n<h4>Case 1 — Vegan eating, fatigue and heavy periods</h4>\n<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Maya, 29, plans pregnancy in six months. She eats beans, grains and vegetables, rarely buys fortified foods, uses no B12 supplement and reports heavy periods and fatigue.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason through it:</strong> Her food pattern offers strengths, but reliable B12 supply is unclear. Heavy bleeding raises concern about iron depletion; fatigue alone does not establish the cause. Ask about meal adequacy and other symptoms rather than assuming that vegan eating itself is the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Plan:</strong> Explain routine folic acid guidance; help identify dependable B12 sources; encourage clinician assessment of bleeding and possible deficiencies. Discuss affordable iron-containing meals, such as lentils with tomatoes or peppers. Do not prescribe high-dose iron solely from this story. Follow up on access to appointments and whether the food/supplement plan is usable.</p>\n<h4>Case 2 — PCOS and an expensive “reset”</h4>\n<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Ana, 34, has PCOS and is offered a costly detox, carbohydrate ban and fertility powder. She feels blamed for irregular cycles.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason through it:</strong> The claimed cure exceeds the evidence. Ask what she hopes the programme will achieve, whether she is skipping meals and whether she has ongoing medical care. Explain that a restrictive diet is not a universal PCOS requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Plan:</strong> Offer a sustainable meal pattern based on her preferences, review the powder’s ingredients and coordinate appropriate clinical care. An achievable first goal might be a reliable lunch three workdays a week, not a promise to “balance hormones” within a month. Validate that seeking support is reasonable and infertility is not a moral failure.</p>\n<h4>Case 3 — Diabetes and the “perfect prenatal”</h4>\n<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Sam, 31, has type 2 diabetes and wants to conceive soon. They ask which prenatal will make pregnancy safe.</p>\n<p><strong>Reason through it:</strong> Supplement choice is only one component. Ask whether a preconception appointment is arranged and whether the prescribing team knows the pregnancy plan. Avoid giving medication instructions or guaranteeing safety based on one glucose result.</p>\n<p><strong>Plan:</strong> Coordinate clinician-led diabetes planning, review medicines and supplements, and support workable meals within that plan. Explain folic acid and ask whether individualized higher-risk advice is needed. The immediate success measure is connection to an appropriate plan, not finding the most expensive vitamin.</p>","id":"l2-10"},{"title":"Targeted teaching for your supplied 20-question test","html":"<p>Your uploaded <strong>Lesson 2 Test Questions — Nutritional Counseling, 7th Edition</strong> is available. This section maps its topics to current explanations; no official answer key or complete textbook chapter was supplied. Some questions use broad, dated or internally inconsistent wording. Learn the concept and keep the caveat beside it rather than memorizing an error.</p>\n<h4>Cycle vocabulary and reproductive hormones — Q3, Q10, Q11, Q13</h4>\n<p>The <strong>follicular phase</strong> runs from the start of menstruation to ovulation; the <strong>luteal phase</strong> follows ovulation. FSH and LH are pituitary hormones. Estrogen and progesterone are ovarian hormones, with progesterone particularly associated with the corpus luteum after ovulation. In the test’s explicitly simplified 28-day example, the LH surge is associated with ovulation around day 14. This is a teaching model, not a universal schedule. <a href=\"https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/menstruation/conditioninfo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NICHD: menstruation</a>; <a href=\"https://womenshealth.gov/pregnancy/menstrual-cycle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Office on Women’s Health: menstrual cycle</a></p>\n<p>Uterine <strong>prostaglandins</strong> contribute to contractions and menstrual cramps. They are not another name for progesterone. Pain that is severe, new or disruptive warrants assessment rather than being dismissed as inevitable. <a href=\"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/dysmenorrhea-painful-periods\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ACOG: dysmenorrhea</a></p>\n<p>For Q11, separate <strong>function</strong> from <strong>anatomy</strong>: hormonal disturbances can affect ovulation, cycle phases and sperm production; structural blockage or scarring of reproductive passageways is a different mechanism. The phrase “alterations in passageways” is broad—hormones also influence tract function—so the intended textbook distinction needs checking rather than treating every possible interpretation as equivalent.</p>\n<h4>Adipose tissue, weight and terminology — Q4–Q7, Q9, Q12, Q18</h4>\n<p>Adipose tissue is metabolically active, not merely storage. It produces signals such as leptin and participates in sex-steroid metabolism. <strong>Insulin is produced by the pancreas</strong>, not by fat cells. Q4’s grouping of estrogen, testosterone and leptin as simply “produced by fat cells” compresses different processes; distinguish steroid conversion from primary glandular secretion. Central adiposity is associated with insulin resistance, meaning tissues respond less effectively to insulin. <a href=\"https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hormones-and-endocrine-function/endocrine-related-organs-and-hormones\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Endocrine Society: endocrine-related organs</a>; <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/what-is-diabetes/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIDDK: diabetes overview</a></p>\n<p>Weight-loss interventions can improve some metabolic or ovulatory outcomes in selected populations, but improved hormones or oxidative-stress markers do not guarantee conception. In ovulatory women with obesity, preconception weight-loss interventions have not been shown to improve live birth after infertility treatment. Q5’s broad wording should not be turned into a promise. <a href=\"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/obesity-and-reproduction-a-committee-opinion-2021/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASRM: obesity and reproduction</a></p>\n<p>Low prepregnancy weight is associated with greater risk of low birth weight and preterm birth; that is not the same mechanism as folate-sensitive neural tube defects. A BMI number alone cannot establish normal reproductive function, and athlete status does not specify a universal menarche delay. Q6’s threshold and Q12’s exact duration need the textbook context; neither is a diagnostic rule. <a href=\"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/good-health-before-pregnancy-prepregnancy-care\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ACOG: prepregnancy care</a></p>\n<p><strong>Subfertility</strong> describes reduced fertility; <strong>infertility</strong> has formal clinical definitions rather than meaning “absolutely impossible to conceive.” WHO uses failure to achieve pregnancy after at least 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse, while evaluation may occur earlier according to age/history. Q7’s examples suggest impaired reproductive capacity but do not supply enough history for an individual diagnosis. At age 35 or older, clinicians often evaluate after six months rather than waiting a year. <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/infertility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WHO: infertility</a>; <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/reproductive-health/infertility-faq/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CDC: infertility FAQs</a></p>\n<h4>Miscarriage and PMDD — Q1, Q2, Q14</h4>\n<p>A miscarriage percentage depends on whether the denominator includes all conceptions or only recognized pregnancies and on the gestational period studied. ACOG reports early pregnancy loss in about 10% of clinically recognized pregnancies, with approximately half attributed to fetal chromosomal abnormalities. Q1 offers 6–9% for a different stated window: none can be verified as the course answer without its source. Q2’s “severe defect” is less precise than chromosomal abnormality. These facts do not imply that a person caused the loss through food choices. <a href=\"https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/practice-bulletin/articles/2018/11/early-pregnancy-loss\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ACOG: early pregnancy loss bulletin</a></p>\n<p><strong>PMDD</strong> involves a cyclical pattern of substantial emotional symptoms, potentially including irritability, depressed mood and mood changes, alongside physical symptoms. Functional impairment and timing matter; ordinary irritability alone is not a diagnosis. Clinical assessment can use prospective symptom tracking. <a href=\"https://womenshealth.gov/menstrual-cycle/premenstrual-syndrome/premenstrual-dysphoric-disorder-pmdd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Office on Women’s Health: PMDD</a></p>\n<h4>Maternal PKU and hyperglycaemia — Q15, Q16</h4>\n<p><strong>Phenylketonuria (PKU)</strong> affects phenylalanine metabolism. High maternal phenylalanine can harm fetal development, including increased risk of microcephaly, congenital heart defects and intellectual disability, even when the fetus does not itself have PKU. Preconception and pregnancy care require a specialist metabolic team and prescribed nutrition management—not a generic high-protein or self-directed elimination diet. The test uses an outdated disability term; use <strong>intellectual disability</strong> in your own explanations. <a href=\"https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pku/conditioninfo/Pages/faqs.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NICHD: maternal PKU</a></p>\n<p>A <strong>teratogenic exposure</strong> can disrupt development and increase congenital-anomaly risk. For Q16, the relevant exposure is high blood glucose early in pregnancy, not merely the label “type 1” or “type 2.” The test’s fixed two- to three-fold figure requires its source; individual risk depends on context and glucose management. <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/diabetes-pregnancy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIDDK: pregnancy with diabetes</a></p>\n<h4>Glycaemic index and fibre — Q17</h4>\n<p>Glycaemic index compares carbohydrate foods’ effects on blood glucose under standardized conditions. Portion size, the mixed meal and overall eating pattern still matter. Lower-GI options can be part of diabetes nutrition care; they are not a licence to ignore carbohydrate quantity or nutritional quality. <a href=\"https://www.diabetes.ca/for-professionals/glycemic-index\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Diabetes Canada: glycaemic index</a></p>\n<p>Q17 lists both <strong>25–35 g/day</strong> and <strong>14 g/1,000 kcal</strong>. They can overlap: at 2,000 kcal, <strong>14 × 2 = 28 g/day</strong>. Thus the wording does not distinguish these choices cleanly. This is an arithmetic illustration, not an individualized energy or fibre prescription; check the course’s stated recommendation rather than claiming an unambiguous key.</p>","id":"l2-11"},{"title":"Jane’s case: work through Q19 and Q20","html":"<p>The supplied case gives age 36, blood pressure 110/70 mm Hg, fasting glucose 130 mg/dL, HDL 35 mg/dL, triglycerides 175 mg/dL, waist 36 inches, weight 150 lb and height 5 ft 3 in.</p>\n<p><strong>Metabolic syndrome</strong> is a cluster, commonly identified by at least three of five features: increased waist circumference, elevated triglycerides, low HDL, elevated blood pressure and elevated fasting glucose. Common US criteria for women include waist over 35 inches, triglycerides at least 150 mg/dL, HDL below 50 mg/dL, blood pressure at least 130/85 and fasting glucose at least 100 mg/dL; treatment and ethnicity-specific waist thresholds can affect assessment. <a href=\"https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/metabolic-syndrome/symptoms-and-diagnosis-of-metabolic-syndrome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">American Heart Association: metabolic syndrome criteria</a></p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Jane’s finding</th><th scope=\"col\">Reasoning using those criteria</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Waist 36 inches</td><td>Above the cited waist threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Triglycerides 175 mg/dL</td><td>Elevated</td></tr><tr><td>HDL 35 mg/dL</td><td><strong>Low</strong>, not high</td></tr><tr><td>Fasting glucose 130 mg/dL</td><td>Elevated</td></tr><tr><td>Blood pressure 110/70</td><td>Not elevated by that criterion</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Four findings fit the metabolic-syndrome pattern, making it the strongest case interpretation among Q19’s choices. This is educational reasoning, not a diagnosis of a real person. A fasting glucose of 130 mg/dL also exceeds the diabetes diagnostic threshold of 126 mg/dL; absent unequivocal hyperglycaemia, confirmation is required. Therefore “metabolic syndrome” must not distract from glucose follow-up. This is not gestational diabetes because the case does not establish pregnancy. <a href=\"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/tests-diagnosis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIDDK: diabetes tests and diagnosis</a></p>\n<p><strong>Critical Q20 correction:</strong> Option c incorrectly describes HDL as high. The meaningful pattern is <strong>high glucose + high triglycerides + low HDL</strong>, together with waist circumference. None of the printed options expresses that correctly. Do not memorize “high HDL” as the abnormality; flag the wording for the instructor.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra calculation, not a diagnostic substitute:</strong> Height = 63 inches. BMI = 703 × 150 ÷ 63² = <strong>26.6 kg/m²</strong>, approximately. Waist = 36 × 2.54 = <strong>91.4 cm</strong>. BMI is not one of the five criteria above; age and difficulty conceiving are not substitutes for the laboratory criteria either. Always keep measurement, interpretation and diagnosis separate.</p>","id":"l2-12"},{"title":"Your 20-question study map","html":"<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Comparison table\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Test item</th><th scope=\"col\">Where to study</th><th scope=\"col\">What to carry into the test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Section 10: miscarriage</td><td>Rate depends on denominator; exact offered percentage unverified</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Section 10: miscarriage</td><td>Chromosomal abnormalities are a major cause; avoid blame</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sections 2 and 10</td><td>Pituitary FSH/LH versus ovarian hormones</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Section 10: adipose</td><td>Insulin comes from pancreas; steroid wording simplified</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sections 3 and 10</td><td>Metabolic changes do not guarantee conception/live birth</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sections 3 and 10</td><td>No universal BMI fertility threshold; source context needed</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Section 10: terminology</td><td>Reduced fertility versus formal diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Section 4</td><td>400 mcg = 0.4 mg folic acid</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Section 10: low weight</td><td>Preterm/low-birth-weight association, not an NTD mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Section 10: cycle</td><td>Prostaglandins and uterine contractions</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Section 10: cycle</td><td>Hormonal function versus structural causes; wording broad</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Sections 3 and 10</td><td>Energy availability matters; exact athlete delay unverified</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Section 10: cycle</td><td>LH surge around day 14 in the stated model only</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Section 10: PMDD</td><td>Emotional and physical symptoms, cyclicity and impairment</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Section 10: PKU</td><td>Elevated maternal phenylalanine and specialist management</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Sections 7 and 10</td><td>Early hyperglycaemia is the exposure; avoid blanket risk figures</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Section 10: fibre</td><td>14 g/1,000 kcal can equal a value within 25–35 g/day</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Section 10: adipose</td><td>Central adiposity and insulin resistance</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Section 11</td><td>Recognize the metabolic cluster and glucose follow-up</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Section 11</td><td>Printed HDL direction is wrong; no fully correct option</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","id":"l2-13"},{"title":"Original practice — explain your answer before checking","html":"<p>These additional questions assess concepts in this guide; use the supplied-test map as well. They are not copied official items.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Why start preparation before a positive pregnancy test?</strong> Early development can precede recognition; routines and clinical review take time. Waiting misses part of the prevention window, but starting after recognition is still worthwhile.</li><li><strong>Is ovulation always on day 14?</strong> No. Cycles vary. A textbook example is not an individual prediction.</li><li><strong>A client eats nutrient-dense foods but skips meals while training. What distinction matters?</strong> Food quality and enough energy are different. Investigate adequacy and symptoms without diagnosing from appearance.</li><li><strong>Convert 0.4 mg folic acid to mcg.</strong> 0.4 × 1,000 = <strong>400 mcg</strong>. Keep the nutrient form unchanged during unit conversion.</li><li><strong>Does 400 mcg DFE necessarily mean 400 mcg folic acid?</strong> No. DFE adjusts for source bioavailability; check the actual folic acid amount.</li><li><strong>A supplement contains 300 mcg folic acid taken with food. What is its DFE contribution?</strong> 300 ÷ 0.6 = <strong>500 mcg DFE</strong>. This calculation does not show that it meets the Canadian 400 mcg folic-acid prevention recommendation.</li><li><strong>Should everyone planning pregnancy take a 27 mg iron tablet?</strong> No. The 27 mg figure is a pregnancy total-intake RDA, not a universal preconception tablet prescription.</li><li><strong>Does a normal-looking diet rule out B12 problems?</strong> No. Reliable supply, absorption and clinical context matter; appearance is not a nutrient assessment.</li><li><strong>What does choline’s AI mean?</strong> It is an intake reference used when evidence is insufficient for an RDA, not a statement that choline is optional.</li><li><strong>Two high-mercury fish portions are 80 g each in one month. Total and interpretation?</strong> <strong>160 g</strong>, above the Canadian 150 g/month combined limit for the specified group and fish. Do not substitute a weekly limit.</li><li><strong>Caffeine amounts are 150, 80 and 30 mg. Can another 80 mg fit under 300 mg?</strong> Initial <strong>260 mg</strong>; adding 80 gives <strong>340 mg</strong>, above the maximum. Use actual product amounts in real life.</li><li><strong>Why not double a multivitamin when more folic acid is being considered?</strong> All ingredients increase, not just folic acid. Higher-risk dosing needs individual professional assessment.</li><li><strong>Does PCOS require the same low-carbohydrate diet for everyone?</strong> No. The guideline does not establish one superior dietary composition; individual needs and sustainability matter.</li><li><strong>A fertility powder improves a laboratory marker. What key evidence is missing?</strong> Whether it improves meaningful clinical outcomes, such as pregnancy or live birth, with acceptable harms and in relevant populations.</li><li><strong>A client with diabetes asks for a prenatal instead of a preconception appointment. Best response?</strong> Explain that supplements cannot replace glucose, medication and complication planning with the clinical team.</li><li><strong>What makes a counselling goal useful?</strong> It is agreed, feasible, specific and reviewable. “Pack a workable lunch on three shifts this week” is more actionable than “eat perfectly for fertility.”</li></ol>","id":"l2-14"},{"title":"Final retrieval checklist","html":"<p>Close the guide and explain: the two pregnancy clocks; why folic acid begins early; 0.4 mg versus 400 mcg; folic acid versus DFE; RDA versus supplement dose; why inadequate energy is not diagnosed by body size; why vegan eating needs a reliable B12 strategy; the caffeine calculation; the two fish limits; and when medical coordination takes priority.</p>\n<p>For written answers, use <strong>claim → reason → safe application</strong>. Example: “Do not double the multivitamin, because it duplicates all ingredients; arrange individualized folic acid advice and review the current label.” That demonstrates understanding better than repeating a number alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Still to verify against your course:</strong> the assigned textbook pages and instructor’s intended answers where the supplied test is ambiguous. The 20 questions are mapped, but current source-based explanations must not be mistaken for an official answer key.</p>","id":"l2-15"}]}};
